An ingot is a bar of metal, the thing a crucible leaves behind and the thing
a forge eats.

Two numbers matter.  Its size, in units, is how much metal is in it -- a
piece of gear takes as many units as its volume comes to, so a bar of light
metal goes less far than its weight suggests.  Its quality, one to five, is
a record of how cleanly it was smelted, and it decides how badly a smith is
punished for a missed stroke.

An ingot carries whatever stats the thing it was made from carried, though
never its armor.  Those stats pass into whatever is forged from it.

FORGE COMBINE will fold one bar into another of the same metal, making one
larger and no worse than the better of the two.

